WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.730
When I was in middle school, my social studies teacher asked me to enter a writing contest.
上初中时，社会研究课老师让我参加一个写作比赛。

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.060
I said no without thinking.
我想都没想就拒绝了。

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.150
I did not love writing.
我并不热爱写作。

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.240
My family came from Brazil, so English was only my second language.
我家来自巴西，所以英语只是我的第二语言。

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:27.040
Writing was so difficult and painful for me that my teacher had allowed me to present my paper on the sinking of the Titanic by acting out a play, where I played all the parts.
写作对我来说又难又痛苦，所以老师曾允许我用表演一出短剧的方式来提交关于泰坦尼克号沉没的作业，剧中所有角色都由我一个人扮演。

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.580
No one laughed harder than he did.
谁也没有他笑得厉害。

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.210
So, why did he suddenly force me to do something at which I was sure to fail?
那么，他为什么突然逼我去做一件我注定会失败的事呢？

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.580
His reply: “Because I love your stories.
他的回答是：“因为我喜欢你讲的故事。

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.920
If you’re willing to apply yourself, I think you have a good shot at this.”
只要你肯下功夫，我觉得你很有希望。”

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.880
Encouraged by his words, I agreed to give it a try.
受到他这番话的鼓励，我同意试一试。

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.120
I chose Paul Revere’s horse as my subject.
我选择保罗·里维尔的马作为我写作的对象。

00:00:49.920 --> 00:01:00.450
Paul Revere was a silversmith in Boston who rode a horse at night on April 18, 1775, to Lexington to warn people that British soldiers were coming.
保罗·里维尔是波士顿的一名银匠，1775年4月18日夜里，他骑马赶到列克星敦，警告人们英国士兵要来了。

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.640
My story would come straight from the horse’s mouth.
我的故事将由这匹马亲口讲述。

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.490
Not a brilliant idea, but funny; and unlikely to be anyone else’s choice.
算不上什么妙主意，但挺有趣，而且别人不太可能会选。

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.120
What did the horse think, as he sped through the night?
那匹马在夜色中疾驰时，心里在想些什么呢？

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.010
Did he get tired?
它累不累？

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:16.640
Have doubts?
有没有过动摇？

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.530
Did he want to quit?
它想不想放弃？

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.880
I sympathized immediately.
我一下子就感同身受。

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.660
I got tired.
我累了。

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.360
I had doubts.
我也有过动摇。

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.290
I wanted to quit.
我想放弃。

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.690
But, like Revere’s horse, I kept going.
但是，就像里维尔的马一样，我坚持了下来。

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.430
I worked hard.
我很努力。

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.420
I checked my spelling.
我检查了拼写。

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.800
I asked my older sister to correct my grammar.
我请姐姐帮我纠正语法。

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.940
I checked out a half dozen books on Paul Revere from the library.
我从图书馆借了六七本关于保罗·里维尔的书。

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.200
I even read a few of them.
其中几本我甚至还读了。

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.280
When I handed in the essay to my teacher, he read it, laughed out loud, and said, “Great.
我把作文交给老师，他读完后放声大笑，说：“很好。

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.260
Now, write it again.”
现在，再写一遍。”

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.470
I wrote it again, and again and again.
我又写了一遍，一遍又一遍。

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.320
When I finally finished it, the thought of winning had given way to the enjoyment of writing.
等我终于写完时，想赢的念头已经让位给了写作的乐趣。

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.320
If I didn’t win, I wouldn’t care.
就算没赢，我也不在乎。

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.770
A few weeks later, when I had almost forgotten the contest, there came the news.
几周后，我都快把这场比赛忘了，消息却传来了。

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.770
I went to my teacher’s office after the award presentation.
颁奖典礼结束后，我去了老师的办公室。
